import{c as a,$ as i,Q as r,j as s,g as t,o as n,n as c,m as l}from"./chunks/framework.Ce_49mA8.js";const y=JSON.parse('{"title":"Production (Workbench)","description":"Workshop dispatching, managing dynamic assembly queues, cascade material reservation, and finished goods release in OmniCraft ERP.","frontmatter":{"title":"Production (Workbench)","description":"Workshop dispatching, managing dynamic assembly queues, cascade material reservation, and finished goods release in OmniCraft ERP.","datePublished":"2026-11-15","dateModified":"2026-11-15","order":7},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/spaces/production/workbench.md","filePath":"docs/spaces/production/workbench.md","lastUpdated":1794700800000}'),d={name:"docs/spaces/production/workbench.md"},u={id:"production-the-workbench-manufacturing-queue",tabindex:"-1"};function h(p,e,m,g,f,b){const o=i("DocIcon");return r(),s("div",null,[t("h1",u,[n(o,{name:"workbench"}),e[0]||(e[0]=c(" Production: The Workbench & Manufacturing Queue ",-1)),e[1]||(e[1]=t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#production-the-workbench-manufacturing-queue","aria-label":'Permalink to "Production: The Workbench & Manufacturing Queue"'},"​",-1))]),e[2]||(e[2]=l('<p>In craftsmanship and custom manufacturing, friction inevitably arises at the intersection of scheduling and active assembly: if materials are deducted from spreadsheets prematurely, warehouse stock reports misleading deficits for urgent orders; if materials are never reserved, multiple scheduled products compete for the exact same batch of leather, timber, or hardware. As a result, the workshop either halts work due to unexpected stockouts or buries liquid capital in excessive safety buffer stock.</p><p>The <strong>&quot;Production&quot; (Workbench)</strong> tab in <strong>OmniCraft ERP</strong> serves as an intelligent dispatching dashboard for your workshop. All batches scheduled from the <strong>&quot;Calculator&quot;</strong> tab converge here. The primary engineering strength of the Workbench is <strong>dynamic cascade planning without rigid warehouse stock locks</strong>.</p><hr><h2 id="principle-of-soft-material-reservation" tabindex="-1">Principle of Soft Material Reservation <a class="header-anchor" href="#principle-of-soft-material-reservation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Principle of Soft Material Reservation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Unlike rigid enterprise systems where scheduling an order permanently locks warehouse items, OmniCraft ERP employs the concept of a &quot;soft reserve.&quot;</p><p>When clicking the <strong>&quot;Start Production&quot;</strong> button in the Calculator, raw materials <strong>are not physically deducted from warehouse stock</strong>. Instead, the application computes a dynamic virtual reserve in memory, evaluating material availability in real time.</p><p>This provides the craftsman with essential operational flexibility:</p><ul><li>You can outline a production pipeline weeks in advance, even if key raw materials have not yet been delivered or are still in transit from suppliers.</li><li>Inventory records in the <strong>&quot;Materials&quot;</strong> tab remain clean, reflecting exact physical stock sitting on your workshop shelves.</li><li>If customer deadlines change suddenly, any batch can be rearranged in the queue or cancelled instantly without generating tedious return invoices.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="dynamic-queue-and-cascade-prioritization" tabindex="-1">Dynamic Queue and Cascade Prioritization <a class="header-anchor" href="#dynamic-queue-and-cascade-prioritization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dynamic Queue and Cascade Prioritization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Workbench queue operates on a <strong>top-down cascade resource allocation</strong> principle. The system evaluates current physical stock in the warehouse and allocates available quantities sequentially based on each order&#39;s numerical position in the queue.</p><p>Every order card in the active queue displays one of two real-time statuses:</p><ol><li><strong>&quot;✅ Secured with materials&quot;</strong> — the warehouse holds sufficient free stock across all required components, factoring in the needs of all higher-priority orders in the list.</li><li><strong>&quot;⚠️ Material deficit&quot;</strong> — the application explicitly details which materials are missing alongside the exact shortage quantity in standard measurement units (<code>sq ft</code>, <code>lin m</code>, <code>kg</code>, <code>pcs</code>).</li></ol><div class="formula-hero-card"><div class="formula-card-top"><span class="formula-badge">Dispatching Principle</span><div class="formula-heading">Cascade Stock Allocation by Priority Rank</div></div><div class="formula-equation-box"><div class="math-formula-display"><span class="math-var">Free Available Stock</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-var">Physical Inventory</span><span class="math-op">−</span><span class="math-var">Sum of Reserves for Orders Higher in Queue</span></div></div></div><h3 id="practical-case-managing-order-priority" tabindex="-1">Practical Case: Managing Order Priority <a class="header-anchor" href="#practical-case-managing-order-priority" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Practical Case: Managing Order Priority&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Consider a realistic scenario in a leathercraft studio:</p><ul><li>Active warehouse stock shows: <code>100 sq ft</code> of vegetable-tanned leather.</li><li>Two orders are queued on the Workbench: <ol><li><em>Travel Duffel Bag</em> (requires <code>80 sq ft</code> of leather);</li><li><em>Urban Daypack</em> (requires <code>90 sq ft</code> of leather).</li></ol></li></ul><p>If the <em>Duffel Bag</em> sits above the <em>Daypack</em> in the Workbench queue, the system allocates <code>80 sq ft</code> to the Duffel Bag (Status: <strong>Secured with materials</strong>). For the Daypack, only <code>20 sq ft</code> remains, prompting the status: <strong>Material deficit: Leather (70.0 sq ft)</strong>.</p><p>If the Daypack client pays for expedited delivery, the craftsman clicks the <strong>&quot;Move up in queue&quot;</strong> (↑) arrow button on the Daypack card. The application recalculates stock availability from the top down: the Daypack now claims <code>90 sq ft</code> (switching to green secured status), while the Duffel Bag drops below and enters deficit status (<code>70.0 sq ft</code>).</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Master Craftsman Tip: Scheduling Ahead with Zero Stock</p><p>You can confidently schedule orders against expected incoming shipments. As soon as a purchasing invoice is recorded under the Materials tab, red deficit indicators across your Workbench automatically turn green without requiring manual re-entry.</p></div><hr><h2 id="anatomy-of-a-production-order-card" tabindex="-1">Anatomy of a Production Order Card <a class="header-anchor" href="#anatomy-of-a-production-order-card" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Anatomy of a Production Order Card&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each order on the Workbench is rendered as an independent card equipped with comprehensive dispatching controls:</p><ul><li><strong>Queue Reordering Controls:</strong> Dedicated <strong>&quot;Move up in queue&quot;</strong> (↑) and <strong>&quot;Move down in queue&quot;</strong> (↓) arrow buttons. The up arrow is disabled for the first item; the down arrow is disabled for the last.</li><li><strong>Order Information Block:</strong><ul><li>Product name derived from the specification sheet;</li><li>Batch quantity (e.g., <em>“Quantity: 5 pcs”</em>);</li><li>Exact date and time the order was queued;</li><li>Estimated batch cost (calculated from specification consumption norms and active warehouse prices);</li><li>Real-time material allocation status.</li></ul></li><li><strong>AI Order Assistant (spark icon button):</strong> Initiates an automated assessment of the production order. The AI assistant evaluates material readiness, compares estimated costs against batch volume, and provides recommendations on assembly sequencing.</li><li><strong>&quot;Cancel&quot; Button:</strong> Opens a confirmation modal. Once confirmed, the order is removed from the Workbench, and its soft reserve of materials is instantly reallocated to lower-ranked orders.</li><li><strong>&quot;Complete&quot; Button:</strong> Triggers the physical completion process, recording finished stock into inventory.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="finished-goods-release-completing-production" tabindex="-1">Finished Goods Release (Completing Production) <a class="header-anchor" href="#finished-goods-release-completing-production" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Finished Goods Release (Completing Production)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a batch of goods is physically assembled, burnished, inspected, and approved for release, the craftsman clicks the <strong>&quot;Complete&quot;</strong> button.</p><p>At this exact millisecond, the application executes a single atomic transaction within SQLite:</p><ol><li><strong>Physical Raw Materials Deduction:</strong> The system strictly deducts product components (<code>usage_type == &#39;product&#39;</code>). Packaging components (mailer boxes, tissue wrap, tags) remain untouched in stock until the final sale. The inventory log records a deduction entry titled <em>“Consumption: Production [Product] x[Quantity]”</em>.</li><li><strong>Historical Cost Price Snapshot:</strong> The application calculates unit cost at the moment of completion using current moving average cost (MAC) valuations, bench time rates, and overhead multipliers. This unit cost figure is <strong>permanently frozen</strong> for this specific batch.</li><li><strong>Showcase Batch Entry (FIFO):</strong> The newly completed batch is booked into showroom inventory (the &quot;Showcase&quot; tab) under a dedicated record in <code>finished_goods_batches</code> referencing the original production order ID and frozen unit cost.</li><li><strong>Order Finalization:</strong> The order status changes to completed with a completion timestamp, removing it from the active Workbench queue.</li></ol><ol class="craft-list-steps"><li><div><strong>Deduct product raw materials:</strong><br> Record an inventory deduction log without touching packaging stock prematurely. </div></li><li><div><strong>Freeze historical unit cost:</strong><br> Permanently snapshot unit cost (MAC + labor + overheads) for this batch. </div></li><li><div><strong>Book finished goods batch:</strong><br> Add completed units to finished stock with independent FIFO batch tracking. </div></li><li><div><strong>Close production order:</strong><br> Record completion timestamp, mark status as done, and archive the workbench card. </div></li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Inventory Physics: Protecting Cost Valuation via First In, First Out (FIFO)</p><p>Suppose on Monday you manufactured 3 belts using leather purchased at an older wholesale price (batch cost: <code>$25</code> per unit). On Friday, fresh leather arrived at a higher cost, and you manufactured 3 more identical belts (batch cost: <code>$32</code> per unit).</p><p>Your finished goods inventory now holds 6 identical belts. When a customer purchases a belt via the Sales tab, the system will deduct units from the Monday batch at <code>$25</code> first, and subsequently from the Friday batch at <code>$32</code> following the <strong>First In, First Out (FIFO)</strong> method. Your financial profit report reflects authentic commercial margins without arbitrary cost averaging.</p></div><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Continuous Operations: Tolerance for Negative Material Balances</p><p>OmniCraft ERP prioritizes real-world workshop continuity. If a craftsman forgot to enter yesterday&#39;s delivery invoice for adhesives or hardware into the software, the system will not block production completion. The raw material balance slides into a temporary technical negative, allowing completed goods to be booked into stock on time. When the invoice is entered later, inventory balances reconcile automatically.</p></div><hr><h2 id="trial-version-limitations-demo-mode" tabindex="-1">Trial Version Limitations (Demo Mode) <a class="header-anchor" href="#trial-version-limitations-demo-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Trial Version Limitations (Demo Mode)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The free trial version of the software includes a limit of <strong>20 processed commercial transactions (sales receipts)</strong>:</p><ul><li>Once this threshold is reached, a <strong>&quot;Trial Version Limit Reached&quot;</strong> warning banner appears across the top of the Workbench.</li><li>The <strong>&quot;Complete&quot;</strong> button is disabled, shifting the Workbench into a view-only schedule.</li><li>However, reordering queue priorities via the up/down arrows and cancelling pending orders remain fully functional.</li><li>To remove the restriction, simply activate a lifetime license key or active subscription under Workspace Settings.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="related-documentation-cross-links" tabindex="-1">Related Documentation &amp; Cross-Links <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-documentation-cross-links" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related Documentation &amp; Cross-Links&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/docs/spaces/production/calculator">Calculator &amp; Recipes</a> — designing specification sheets, overhead multipliers, and dispatching batches.</li><li><a href="/docs/spaces/production/showcase">Finished Goods Showcase</a> — managing finished inventory, FIFO batches, and manual adjustments.</li><li><a href="/docs/resources/warehouse">Raw Materials &amp; Warehouse</a> — receiving shipments, allocating freight, and moving average cost (MAC).</li><li><a href="/docs/spaces/production/cycle">Production Cycle</a> — end-to-end practical walkthrough from procurement to sales receipt.</li></ul>',39))])}const k=a(d,[["render",h]]);export{y as __pageData,k as default};
